+# Shipping the Tip Jar
+
+The site ships as a **static export pinned on IPFS**, pointed at the `TipJar` contract on **Base mainnet**.
+
+> Nothing here has been uploaded or deployed. This document is the runbook: every command below is meant to be
+> run by a human who has decided to ship, with a funded deployer key. Local development never needs any of it —
+> see [README.md](./README.md).
+
+Two things have to happen in order, because the frontend bakes the contract address in at build time:
+
+1. Deploy `TipJar` to Base → `deployedContracts.ts` gains an `8453` entry.
+2. Build the static site with `NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base` and pin the result.
+
+## 0. Prerequisites
+
+- **Node 20 or 22 LTS** (`nvm use`). Node 25 ships a stub `localStorage` global that breaks Next.js
+  prerendering; the export will fail on it.
+- A deployer account with ETH on Base, as a Foundry keystore:
+  ```bash
+  yarn account:import          # import an existing key
+  yarn generate                # or create a fresh one
+  yarn account                 # show address + balances, confirm it is funded on Base
+  ```
+- `packages/foundry/.env` → real `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` (Etherscan v2 keys cover Base) for contract verification.
+- Optional but recommended for a public site, in `packages/nextjs/.env.local`:
+  ```bash
+  NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY=...          # otherwise the shared Scaffold-ETH demo key is used
+  NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID=...
+  ```
+  These are compiled into the bundle, so changing them means rebuilding and re-pinning.
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+```bash
+yarn deploy --network base
+```
+
+Runs `script/Deploy.s.sol` → `DeployTipJar`, which pins the jar to
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` (Circle USDC on Base) and makes the deployer the jar owner. It
+reverts before spending anything if that address holds no code on the target chain.
+
+Then verify the source on Basescan and confirm the generated address landed in the frontend:
+
+```bash
+yarn verify:base
+grep -A2 '"8453"\|8453:' packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts | head
+```
+
+Sanity-check the live contract before building anything against it:
+
+```bash
+JAR=<address printed by the deploy>
+
+cast call $JAR "token()(address)"       --rpc-url base   # -> 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+cast call $JAR "owner()(address)"       --rpc-url base   # -> your deployer
+cast call $JAR "totalTipped()(uint256)" --rpc-url base   # -> 0
+```
+
+## 2. Build the static site
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs:build
+```
+
+which is exactly:
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true yarn workspace @se-2/nextjs build
+```
+
+- `NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK=base` switches `scaffold.config.ts` from the local fork to Base mainnet.
+- `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true` turns on `output: "export"`, `trailingSlash: true` and unoptimized images in
+  `next.config.ts` — required, because IPFS serves plain files with no Next.js server.
+
+Output: `packages/nextjs/out/` (~9 MB).
+
+## 3. Check the build before uploading
+
+Uploading is the irreversible-ish step (content spreads and gets cached), so check first.
+
+```bash
+JAR=<the Base address from step 1>
+
+# The Base jar address and USDC are actually baked into the bundle
+grep -ril "$JAR" packages/nextjs/out/_next | head
+grep -ril "833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913" packages/nextjs/out/_next | head
+
+# Serve it exactly like a subdomain gateway does (site at the root) and click through it
+npx serve packages/nextjs/out    # -> http://localhost:3000
+```
+
+In the browser: the jar address and owner should match step 1, the feed should load, and connecting a wallet
+should offer to switch to Base. If the page instead says *"No TipJar found on Base"*, the build did not pick up
+a Base deployment — redo step 1 and rebuild.
+
+(Do not bother grepping the bundle for `localhost`: viem ships every chain definition, including the local
+one, so those strings are there in any build. What decides the target network is
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_TARGET_NETWORK`, and the page above is what proves it took effect.)
+
+## 4. Upload and pin
+
+Pick one. All of them must produce a **CIDv1** (base32) — subdomain gateways, which is how this site has to be
+served (see [Gateways](#gateways)), only accept CIDv1.
+
+**Scaffold-ETH's bundled uploader** (builds and uploads in one go):
+
+```bash
+yarn ipfs
+# -> 🚀 Upload complete! Your site is now available at: https://community.bgipfs.com/ipfs/<CID>
+```
+
+**Your own kubo node:**
+
+```bash
+ipfs add -r --cid-version 1 --quieter packages/nextjs/out   # last line is the CID
+ipfs pin add <CID>
+ipfs name publish /ipfs/<CID>                               # optional IPNS pointer
+```
+
+**A pinning service** (so the site stays up when your node is not):
+
+```bash
+# Storacha / web3.storage
+npx @web3-storage/w3cli up packages/nextjs/out
+```
+
+Any other provider (Pinata, Filebase, 4everland, …) works too — follow their CLI docs, upload
+`packages/nextjs/out` as a directory, and check the CID they return is the CIDv1 you computed above.
+
+Record the CID. It is the release identifier: any change to any file produces a different CID, and the old one
+keeps serving the old site until it is unpinned and garbage-collected.
+
+## 5. Post-deploy verification
+
+Run all of these against the CID you just pinned.
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid from step 4>
+JAR=<the Base address from step 1>
+SITE=https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link
+```
+
+**The CID matches what you built** (recomputes the hash locally, uploads nothing):
+
+```bash
+ipfs add -r --only-hash --quieter --cid-version 1 packages/nextjs/out   # must equal $CID
+```
+
+**The site is retrievable and is the right site:**
+
+```bash
+curl -sI  "$SITE/" | head -1                                   # HTTP/2 200
+curl -s   "$SITE/" | grep -o "<title>[^<]*</title>"            # <title>USDC Tip Jar</title>
+curl -sI  "$SITE/debug/" | head -1                             # HTTP/2 200 (trailing slash matters)
+curl -sI  "$SITE/favicon.png" | head -1                        # HTTP/2 200
+```
+
+**The JS actually points at the Base jar** — pull every chunk the page loads and look for the address:
+
+```bash
+curl -s "$SITE/" | grep -o '/_next/static/[^"]*\.js' | sort -u \
+  | while read -r path; do curl -s "$SITE$path"; done | grep -c "$JAR"    # >= 1
+```
+
+**It is pinned somewhere durable**, not just on your laptop:
+
+```bash
+ipfs pin ls --type=recursive "$CID"          # your node
+ipfs routing findprovs "$CID" | head         # who else is serving it
+```
+
+...plus the CID showing up in your pinning service's own dashboard or `list` command.
+
+**It resolves from a gateway you do not control** (fresh cache, no local node):
+
+```bash
+curl -sI "https://$CID.ipfs.w3s.link/"        | head -1
+curl -sI "https://$CID.ipfs.inbrowser.link/"  | head -1
+```
+
+**The contract behind it is live:**
+
+```bash
+cast code $JAR --rpc-url base | wc -c                      # > 2
+cast call $JAR "token()(address)" --rpc-url base           # USDC on Base
+```
+
+Finally, in a browser on the gateway URL: connect a wallet, confirm it prompts for **Base**, send one small
+real tip (a few cents of USDC), and confirm it shows up in the feed. That is the only check that exercises the
+whole path end to end.
+
+If a DNSLink domain fronts the site, update it last, once the CID is verified:
+
+```bash
+# TXT _dnslink.tips.example.com = "dnslink=/ipfs/<CID>"
+dig +short TXT _dnslink.tips.example.com
+```
+
+## Gateways
+
+The export uses absolute asset URLs (`/_next/static/...`), so the site must be served from the **root of an
+origin**:
+
+- ✅ subdomain gateway — `https://<cidv1>.ipfs.dweb.link/`
+- ✅ DNSLink domain — `https://tips.example.com/`
+- ⚠️ path gateway — `https://ipfs.io/ipfs/<cid>/` resolves assets to `https://ipfs.io/_next/...` and 404s.
+  Some gateways redirect CIDv1 path requests to their subdomain form and happen to work; do not rely on it.
+
+Next.js cannot emit relative asset URLs that survive nested routes (`/debug/` would look for
+`/debug/_next/...`), so a subdomain gateway or DNSLink domain is the supported way to serve this build.
+
+## Notes
+
+- **The tip feed queries logs directly** from the deployment block via `useScaffoldEventHistory`. That is fine
+  for a young jar, but it walks the chain in 500-block batches and will get slow and RPC-heavy as Base moves
+  on. Before this gets real traffic, put an indexer behind the feed (`.agents/skills/ponder`) or set a
+  dedicated RPC via `NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY`.
+- **Nothing about the site is upgradeable in place.** Shipping a fix means rebuild → new CID → re-pin →
+  update DNSLink. The contract is not upgradeable either: a new jar is a new address, and the old jar keeps
+  whatever it holds until its owner withdraws.
+- **Rolling back** is repointing DNSLink at the previous CID (keep the last known-good one pinned).
